WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.730
Every time a student opens the university

2
00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:06.330
portal, their browser agrees a secret key

3
00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:09.250
with a server it has never met, over a

4
00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:11.230
network anyone can listen to.

5
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.020
How is that possible?

6
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:17.200
Today: how two strangers agree a

7
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:20.700
secret in public, how RSA encrypts and

8
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.260
signs, and how to teach the number

9
00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.060
theory without losing the class.

10
00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:28.610
Public-key cryptography rests on

11
00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:30.690
functions that are easy one way

12
00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:32.210
and hard to reverse.

13
00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.900
Multiplying two large primes is easy.

14
00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.080
Factoring the product is

15
00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:39.420
believed to be hard.

16
00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:43.130
So is undoing modular exponentiation.

17
00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:47.180
Alice and Bob publicly agree a

18
00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.900
prime p and a base g.

19
00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:52.390
Each picks a secret and

20
00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:54.070
sends g to that power.

21
00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:56.600
Each then raises what they

22
00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.660
received to their own secret.

23
00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:00.950
Both reach the same key,

24
00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:02.650
and a listener cannot.

25
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:06.560
With p twenty three and g five,

26
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.860
Alice picks six and sends eight.

27
00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:13.350
Bob picks fifteen and sends nineteen.

28
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.360
Both compute two as the shared key.

29
00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:20.490
RSA picks two primes and publishes

30
00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:23.050
their product n with an exponent e.

31
00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:26.860
Only the owner knows d, which undoes e.

32
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.820
Anyone can encrypt with the public key.

33
00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:33.610
Signing reverses the roles: only the

34
00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:36.530
owner can sign, and anyone can verify.

35
00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.740
Start with the paint-mixing analogy:

36
00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:44.940
mixing colours is easy, unmixing is hard.

37
00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:47.610
Then let students run

38
00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:51.570
Diffie–Hellman by hand with tiny primes.

39
00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.660
The common misconception is that

40
00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:58.220
encryption alone proves who sent a

41
00:01:58.220 --> 00:01:58.600
message.

42
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:00.040
It does not.

43
00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:01.890
Quick check!

44
00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:04.430
Bob encrypts a message with

45
00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:05.890
Alice's public key.

46
00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.720
Who can decrypt it?

47
00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:11.070
Only Alice, with her private key.

48
00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:13.730
Not even Bob can decrypt it afterwards.

49
00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.410
But Alice cannot tell it came

50
00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:18.730
from Bob, because anyone could

51
00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:19.770
have used her public key.

52
00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.620
In the app, open the public-key lab.

53
00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.490
Make a small RSA key pair, sign a

54
00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:30.490
message, change one letter and watch the

55
00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:34.270
check fail, then pick bigger primes and

56
00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:36.630
see the work of splitting n grow.

57
00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:38.550
Your turn.

58
00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:41.650
Plain Diffie–Hellman has

59
00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:43.030
no authentication.

60
00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.200
What can an attacker who

61
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.060
controls the network do?

62
00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.490
Run a separate exchange with each

63
00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:53.130
side and relay everything.

64
00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.680
Remember: one-way functions make public

65
00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:59.780
keys possible, signatures prove origin,

66
00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:02.840
and key exchange needs authentication.

67
00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:06.810
Try it in your next lecture: put students

68
00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:09.730
in threes as Alice, Bob and Eve.

69
00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:12.820
They run Diffie–Hellman with p twenty

70
00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:16.020
three on paper, and Eve tries to find

71
00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:17.560
the key from what she hears.
